<!-- CHAPTER 10 | Source of All: Mula Nakshatra, Kṛṣṇa as Root of Existence -->
<div class="draft-container">
  <!-- VERSE SECTION -->
  <div class="verse-section">
    <h2>Bhagavad Gita Chapter 10, Verse 8</h2>
    <div class="sanskrit">
      अहं सर्वस्य प्रभवो मत्त: सर्वं प्रवर्तते ।
      इति मत्वा भजन्ते मां बुधा भावसमन्विता: ॥ ८ ॥
    </div>
    <p>&nbsp;</p>
    
    <div class="transliteration">
      ahaṁ sarvasya prabhavo<br>
      mattaḥ sarvaṁ pravartate<br>
      iti matvā bhajante māṁ<br>
      budhā bhāva-samanvitāḥ
    </div>
    <p>&nbsp;</p>
    
    <div class="translation"><strong>I am the source of all spiritual and material worlds. Everything emanates from Me. The wise who perfectly know this engage in My devotional service and worship Me with all their hearts.</strong></div>
    <p>&nbsp;</p>
    
    <p class="commentary">
Today is Śukla Trayodaśī (13th lunar day of the waxing moon) — an auspicious tithi building toward full moon illumination. The nakṣatra is Mūla (until 12:58 AM tomorrow), meaning "the root" or "foundation," ruled by Nirṛti (goddess of dissolution and alchemical transformation). This morning's Indra yoga (until 12:34 PM) transitions to Vaidhṛti yoga, reflecting the shift from divine sovereignty to sustaining power. Today is Ravivāra (Sunday), governed by Sūrya (the Sun), the planet of vitality, leadership, and divine illumination.

BG 10.8 reveals Kṛṣṇa as the very source (prabhava) of all existence — the ultimate foundation from which everything emanates. Mūla nakshatra, literally "the root," aligns perfectly with this truth: just as a tree's unseen roots sustain its visible form, Kṛṣṇa is the invisible ground of all manifestation. Nirṛti, Mūla's deity, governs the destructive aspect necessary for new growth — the dissolution of old forms so that life can regenerate from its root. This mirrors the Gita's teaching that recognizing Kṛṣṇa as the source leads to buddhi (intelligence) fixed in devotion — the wise (budhāḥ) who know this truth engage in bhakti with their whole being.

The purport emphasizes that learned scholars who understand Kṛṣṇa as the origin of Brahmā, Śiva, and all demigods become fixed in devotional service, unshakable by nonsensical commentaries. All Vedic literature agrees: Kṛṣṇa is the source. From Nārāyaṇa (an expansion of Kṛṣṇa) come Brahmā, the patriarchs, Indra, the Vasus, the Rudras, and the Ādityas — the entire cosmic hierarchy flows from Him as the root.

Today's panchanga illuminates this verse:
- **Tithi:** Śukla Trayodaśī — auspicious for spiritual practices, especially those involving completion and fulfillment. The waxing moon's near-full light supports increasing clarity and devotion.
- **Nakṣatra:** Mūla (until 12:58 AM tomorrow) — "the root," ruled by Nirṛti (goddess of dissolution and transformation). Mūla's energy is about getting to the foundation, digging up what is hidden, and transforming through destruction of the old. This matches BG 10.8's revelation of Kṛṣṇa as the ultimate root/source.
- **Yoga:** Indra (until 12:34 PM), then Vaidhṛti — Indra (king/sovereign) shifts to Vaidhṛti (sustaining power), reflecting the journey from recognizing divine authority to experiencing its nourishing presence. Indra's vajra (thunderbolt) breaks illusion; Vaidhṛti's power maintains what is real.
- **Vāra:** Ravivāra (Sunday) — Sūrya's day. Surya governs the soul (ātman), vitality, and divine illumination. Just as the sun is the root of the solar system's energy, Kṛṣṇa is the root of all existence. Sunday's energy supports practices that connect to the inner source.
- **Special:** Gandamool Nakshatra (Mūla today) — considered highly transformative but requiring awareness. Sarvārtha Siddhi Yoga (until 12:58 AM tomorrow) — "accomplishment of all purposes," supporting the fulfillment that comes from knowing one's root in the Divine.
- **Rāhu Kāla:** 6:25 PM – 8:12 PM — a reminder to protect one's connection to the source during inauspicious periods by fixing attention on Kṛṣṇa as the unchanging root.

The sāṅkhya insight is profound: Prakṛti manifests with innumerable forms and names, but puruṣa realizes itself by tracing all manifestations back to their single source — Kṛṣṇa. As the Yoga Sutras state (YS 1.24): kleśa-karma-vipākāśayair aparāmṛṣṭaḥ puruṣa-viśeṣa Īśvaraḥ — Īśvara is a special puruṣa untouched by afflictions, karma, or fruition. This directly echoes BG 10.8: Kṛṣṇa is the source untouched by the modifications of prakṛti. YS 1.25 adds that Īśvara is omniscient (sarvajña) — the all-knowing source. YS 1.26 notes Īśvara is beyond time — the eternal guru, perfectly matching "the wise who know this engage in My devotional service."

For the bhakti-yogī, this is not abstract philosophy but urgent practice: in a world of branching distractions, the mendicant's weapon is returning to the root. To know Kṛṣṇa as the source is to cut through the illusion of multiplicity. In Mūla's transformative light, cut through superficial identifications with the sword of discernment. In Surya's illuminating gaze, establish your identity eternally rooted in the Divine. The wise do not chase branches; they drink from the root.
    </p>
    <p>&nbsp;</p>
    
    <p class="verse-header">PRACTICE FOR TODAY</p>
    <p><strong>Root Meditation Sadhana:</strong> Before sunrise (during Brahma muhūrta, 4:17–5:05 AM) or during the auspicious Abhijit muhūrta (12:33–1:31 PM), sit facing east and meditate on Kṛṣṇa as the source of your breath, thoughts, and actions. Feel that all your experiences emanate from this one root. Let each breath be a conscious offering — not mechanical repetition, but bhāva-samanvitāḥ (with full feeling and awareness).</p>
    <p><strong>Mūla Discernment:</strong> Since the nakṣatra is Mūla (the root, foundation) until 12:58 AM tomorrow, before each important action, ask: "Does this align with my root? Is this expression flowing from my true source in Kṛṣṇa? Or am I operating from a superficial branch?" Let discrimination (viveka) sharpen like Nirṛti's transformative power.</p>
    <p><strong>Surya's Illumination:</strong> Honor Surya's energy by seeking clarity in your sadhana. If you feel disconnected, do not despair — simply turn your attention back to the source with sincerity. Surya rewards consistent effort to stay rooted in the Divine, not perfection. Let your remembrance be like the sun: steadily illuminating your true nature.</p>
    <p>&nbsp;</p>
    
    <p class="verse-header">TERM DEFINITIONS:</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>aham</strong> (pronoun) — I; the Supreme Personality of Godhead</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>sarvasya</strong> (genitive singular) — of all, of everything</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>prabhavo</strong> (noun) — the source of generation, origin, root</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>mattaḥ</strong> (pronoun) — from Me</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>sarvaṁ</strong> (accusative singular) — everything, all things</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>pravartate</strong> (verb) — emanates, proceeds, flows forth</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>iti</strong> (adverb) — thus, in this way</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>matvā</strong> (gerund) — knowing, understanding</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>bhajante</strong> (verb) — become devoted, worship, engage in service</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>māṁ</strong> (accusative) — unto Me</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>budhāḥ</strong> (noun plural) — the wise, the learned, the intelligent</p>
    <p class="term-definition"><strong>bhāva-samanvitāḥ</strong> (compound) — with great feeling, with full awareness and devotion</p>
